WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.990
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:09.890
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.840
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:14.629
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.210
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:19.200
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.910
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:23.999
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.970
are going to be talking about Italy.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.710
And specifically, the Italian economy.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:30.549
And even more specifically,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:31.920
what went wrong?

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.650
Italy has experienced three decades of

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.419
stagnation, with barely any growth in

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.550
wages or living standards, whilst much of

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.949
the rest of Europe has fared much better.

00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:46.009
Today, we will be talking

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.639
about the reasons why.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.819
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:52.259
minute and get right into it.

00:00:53.619 --> 00:00:57.009
If you were to design the perfect European

00:00:57.009 --> 00:00:59.559
country, from the point of view of giving

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:03.089
it inherent, natural economic advantages,

00:01:03.589 --> 00:01:06.139
a few things might spring to mind.

00:01:06.819 --> 00:01:08.549
It would have to be relatively

00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:10.599
central, with easy connections

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:12.569
to the rest of the continent.

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:15.059
Ideally it would have a good amount

00:01:15.099 --> 00:01:17.819
of coastline, so it was simple to

00:01:18.089 --> 00:01:21.059
import and export goods by sea.

00:01:21.709 --> 00:01:23.649
You would give it a favourable climate,

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:26.539
warm enough to grow high-value crops,

00:01:26.809 --> 00:01:30.029
let's say grapes for wine or olives for

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:34.439
oil, but not so hot that it is unbearable.

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:37.289
The climate would also be nice

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.980
enough so that people would

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.849
want to come there on holiday.

00:01:41.309 --> 00:01:43.259
It would be warm in the summer,

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.469
there would be beaches and places

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:47.740
to relax, but you'd also like

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.519
there to be mountains for people to

00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:52.409
hike up or ski down in the winter.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:54.529
You'd also want other

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.039
reasons for people to visit.

00:01:56.459 --> 00:01:57.829
Sites of historic

00:01:57.829 --> 00:01:58.950
importance would be good.

00:01:59.309 --> 00:02:01.249
Museums, art galleries,

00:02:01.449 --> 00:02:04.199
churches, beautiful old cities.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:05.819
If the food was good,

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:07.339
that would be nice too.

00:02:07.699 --> 00:02:10.299
And if the people tended to be

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:13.217
friendly, outgoing, and welcoming,

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:15.469
well, that would be another bonus.

00:02:16.499 --> 00:02:19.389
You'd also want people to associate the

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:21.909
country with high quality workmanship,

00:02:22.169 --> 00:02:25.219
so that if people knew that a bag

00:02:25.298 --> 00:02:27.769
or a suit was made in that country,

00:02:28.049 --> 00:02:30.499
it would command a premium price.

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:33.779
You would, essentially, create a

00:02:33.799 --> 00:02:37.329
country that looks very much like Italy.

00:02:37.979 --> 00:02:40.959
So, given all these inherent

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:44.069
advantages, why has Italy, and the

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:47.139
Italian economy, fared so poorly?

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:50.359
Why is the average Italian no better

00:02:50.369 --> 00:02:53.909
off than they were 30 years ago, and is

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:56.592
there anything that can be done about it?

00:02:57.039 --> 00:02:58.999
Now, let me start with

00:02:58.999 --> 00:03:00.349
a little disclaimer.

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:02.929
It is hard to find a bigger

00:03:02.929 --> 00:03:04.309
fan of Italy than me.

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:06.979
I have lived in Italy, I am married to

00:03:06.979 --> 00:03:09.189
an Italian, my kids are half-Italian,

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.299
I speak Italian every day at home,

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:13.729
and I am now an Italian citizen.

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:17.009
I come to this subject as someone who has

00:03:17.009 --> 00:03:19.889
a deep love and appreciation for Italy

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:22.859
as a country, and Italians as a people.

00:03:23.289 --> 00:03:25.319
So, with that out of the way,

00:03:25.699 --> 00:03:27.269
let's talk about what went wrong.

00:03:27.969 --> 00:03:30.939
Well, the first important thing to stress

00:03:31.139 --> 00:03:34.519
is that Italy is a relatively new country.

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:37.429
It was, as you may know, a collection

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:40.729
of smaller states until 1861, when

00:03:40.729 --> 00:03:42.919
it became the Kingdom of Italy.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:47.249
And in those early years it was a

00:03:47.289 --> 00:03:50.509
poor, largely agricultural economy.

00:03:50.929 --> 00:03:53.889
The chaos of fascism and the Second World

00:03:53.889 --> 00:03:57.859
War didn't do much to help, and in 1945,

00:03:58.113 --> 00:04:00.943
the country was practically in ruins.

00:04:01.203 --> 00:04:04.173
Bridges were down, factories had been

00:04:04.183 --> 00:04:07.643
bombed, and much of the south lived

00:04:07.653 --> 00:04:10.693
on small-scale farming in conditions

00:04:10.863 --> 00:04:13.053
that had barely changed in a century.

00:04:14.543 --> 00:04:16.593
What followed was one of the

00:04:16.593 --> 00:04:18.703
most extraordinary economic

00:04:18.723 --> 00:04:20.903
transformations in modern history.

00:04:21.683 --> 00:04:23.993
Italians call it il miracolo

00:04:23.993 --> 00:04:26.563
economico, the economic miracle.

00:04:27.373 --> 00:04:30.213
For more than a decade, from the

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.493
early 1950s to the early 1960s,

00:04:34.103 --> 00:04:36.863
the Italian economy boomed, growing

00:04:37.053 --> 00:04:39.483
at almost six per cent a year.

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:43.123
Italy became the workshop of Europe.

00:04:43.663 --> 00:04:46.273
Fiat made small, affordable

00:04:46.273 --> 00:04:48.453
cars like the Fiat 500.

00:04:48.923 --> 00:04:52.243
Piaggio made the Vespa scooter, which you

00:04:52.243 --> 00:04:55.043
can hear about in episode 120, by the way.

00:04:55.803 --> 00:04:58.913
Olivetti made typewriters so beautiful

00:04:59.153 --> 00:05:01.073
you'll still find them in art museums.

00:05:01.273 --> 00:05:03.013
Italian fridges, washing

00:05:03.013 --> 00:05:05.163
machines and shoes poured into

00:05:05.163 --> 00:05:07.293
homes across the continent.

00:05:07.923 --> 00:05:10.603
Millions of people left the poorer

00:05:10.633 --> 00:05:13.733
south and moved north, to the factories

00:05:13.983 --> 00:05:16.433
of cities like Turin and Milan.

00:05:16.972 --> 00:05:19.023
A country of farmers became a

00:05:19.043 --> 00:05:21.403
country of factory workers and,

00:05:21.403 --> 00:05:23.623
increasingly, of consumers.

00:05:24.962 --> 00:05:26.433
All this growth had a

00:05:26.463 --> 00:05:28.003
particular character.

00:05:28.883 --> 00:05:32.003
Rather than being driven by a handful of

00:05:32.073 --> 00:05:35.653
giant corporations, much of Italy's growth

00:05:35.933 --> 00:05:38.863
was built on thousands upon thousands

00:05:39.113 --> 00:05:42.543
of small family firms, often clustered

00:05:42.543 --> 00:05:45.313
together in specialist districts: one

00:05:45.313 --> 00:05:47.813
town making ceramic tiles, another

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:50.793
making spectacle frames, another making

00:05:50.793 --> 00:05:53.203
a particular type of clothing, and so on.

00:05:54.393 --> 00:05:56.943
These firms were flexible,

00:05:57.343 --> 00:06:00.423
hard-working, and brilliant at design.

00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:04.253
They were also assisted by the Italian

00:06:04.253 --> 00:06:07.363
government, which frequently devalued

00:06:07.403 --> 00:06:09.763
the lira, its currency, to make

00:06:09.773 --> 00:06:12.433
Italian exports more competitive.

00:06:13.703 --> 00:06:16.153
When Italian products looked like

00:06:16.153 --> 00:06:17.993
they were becoming too expensive

00:06:18.163 --> 00:06:20.273
for foreign buyers, the government

00:06:20.483 --> 00:06:22.963
would let the lira fall in value.

00:06:23.503 --> 00:06:25.583
A weaker lira made Italian

00:06:25.583 --> 00:06:27.803
goods cheaper abroad, so the

00:06:27.803 --> 00:06:29.863
orders would come flooding back.

00:06:31.313 --> 00:06:33.753
This caused inflation at home,

00:06:33.803 --> 00:06:36.163
in Italy, because imports

00:06:36.453 --> 00:06:37.973
suddenly got more expensive.

00:06:38.473 --> 00:06:41.203
But it kept the factories busy

00:06:41.523 --> 00:06:43.263
and the economy chugging away.

00:06:44.533 --> 00:06:46.963
On the surface, all was well.

00:06:47.433 --> 00:06:49.953
But two problems were growing.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:52.862
The first was debt.

00:06:53.742 --> 00:06:56.573
Now, Italian politics is a topic

00:06:56.573 --> 00:06:58.703
for another day, but over the

00:06:58.703 --> 00:07:00.863
years, politicians offered more

00:07:00.923 --> 00:07:03.323
and more benefits: better pensions,

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:05.883
more public-sector jobs, subsidies

00:07:05.883 --> 00:07:07.563
for this and subsidies for that.

00:07:08.323 --> 00:07:10.793
Of course, these benefits had to

00:07:10.803 --> 00:07:13.523
be paid for, and successive Italian

00:07:13.523 --> 00:07:16.103
governments borrowed more and more money.

00:07:17.083 --> 00:07:20.843
By the early 1990s, Italy's public debt

00:07:21.133 --> 00:07:24.246
had passed one hundred per cent of GDP.

00:07:24.853 --> 00:07:26.763
In other words, the Italian

00:07:26.763 --> 00:07:29.383
state owed more than the entire

00:07:29.393 --> 00:07:31.093
economy produced in a year.

00:07:32.203 --> 00:07:34.633
Servicing this debt meant paying

00:07:34.742 --> 00:07:36.443
interest to creditors, and

00:07:36.453 --> 00:07:38.433
the bill increased every year.

00:07:39.113 --> 00:07:42.233
This "miraculous" economy looked to be

00:07:42.233 --> 00:07:45.903
built on some shaky foundations, and, to

00:07:45.913 --> 00:07:49.253
cut a long story short, in the 1990s, the

00:07:49.253 --> 00:07:52.533
vultures were well and truly circling.

00:07:53.223 --> 00:07:56.683
But out of all this financial chaos, it

00:07:56.683 --> 00:07:59.213
looked like there might be a solution.

00:07:59.673 --> 00:08:01.973
At the end of the decade, a group of

00:08:01.993 --> 00:08:04.393
European countries were planning to get

00:08:04.433 --> 00:08:07.943
rid of their own currencies and adopt a

00:08:07.943 --> 00:08:11.423
new European single currency, the euro.

00:08:12.563 --> 00:08:15.433
This seemed to many in Italy like a

00:08:15.463 --> 00:08:18.263
sensible proposition; not only could

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:20.013
you use the same currency to buy

00:08:20.013 --> 00:08:22.343
goods in Germany, France or Spain,

00:08:22.943 --> 00:08:25.843
but Italy's borrowing costs would be

00:08:25.853 --> 00:08:28.003
cheaper and it wouldn't be constantly

00:08:28.003 --> 00:08:29.783
fighting to support the lira.

00:08:30.933 --> 00:08:33.973
To qualify to join, however, Italy

00:08:34.163 --> 00:08:36.423
had to get its finances in order.

00:08:37.163 --> 00:08:40.823
So through the 1990s, governments raised

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:43.973
taxes and cut spending, year after year.

00:08:44.793 --> 00:08:47.613
And… it worked, in the narrow sense.

00:08:47.983 --> 00:08:49.923
Italy duly joined the euro

00:08:49.993 --> 00:08:52.483
as a founding member in 1999.

00:08:52.953 --> 00:08:55.393
But joining the euro meant

00:08:55.403 --> 00:08:57.253
giving up a tool that Italy had

00:08:57.253 --> 00:08:59.363
used for decades: being able to

00:08:59.623 --> 00:09:02.033
devalue its currency on a whim.

00:09:03.393 --> 00:09:06.843
With the euro, Italian goods and German

00:09:06.843 --> 00:09:09.493
goods were priced in the same currency.

00:09:09.993 --> 00:09:11.753
An Italian government couldn't

00:09:11.753 --> 00:09:14.513
just say "let's devalue the euro

00:09:14.513 --> 00:09:15.963
to make our cars more competitive

00:09:15.963 --> 00:09:18.923
internationally"; any devaluation of

00:09:18.923 --> 00:09:22.243
the euro would also make German or

00:09:22.243 --> 00:09:24.143
French manufactured goods cheaper.

00:09:24.813 --> 00:09:27.363
Suddenly, there was no magic button

00:09:27.573 --> 00:09:29.393
Italian economists could press.

00:09:29.473 --> 00:09:31.976
No way to make exports cheap again.

00:09:32.526 --> 00:09:34.646
None of this would have mattered

00:09:35.066 --> 00:09:37.366
had Italian firms become more

00:09:37.366 --> 00:09:39.346
productive, had they produced

00:09:39.386 --> 00:09:41.196
more economic value per hour.

00:09:42.026 --> 00:09:43.696
The thing is, they didn't

00:09:43.696 --> 00:09:44.526
get more productive.

00:09:45.426 --> 00:09:49.376
Since the mid-1990s, Italian productivity

00:09:49.736 --> 00:09:54.686
has barely grown — between 1999 and 2019,

00:09:55.156 --> 00:09:57.586
the amount an Italian worker produced per

00:09:57.636 --> 00:10:01.996
hour grew by about five per cent in total.

00:10:02.556 --> 00:10:05.806
Five per cent over two whole decades.

00:10:06.426 --> 00:10:08.986
In France and Germany over the same

00:10:08.986 --> 00:10:11.886
period, it grew by more than twenty per

00:10:11.886 --> 00:10:14.776
cent, and they aren't exactly shining

00:10:14.786 --> 00:10:17.276
beacons of economic productivity either.

00:10:17.976 --> 00:10:20.046
In Poland, it rose by

00:10:20.046 --> 00:10:21.336
about sixty per cent.

00:10:21.536 --> 00:10:24.036
In Lithuania, it more than doubled.

00:10:24.196 --> 00:10:27.236
And in Latvia, it rose by even more.

00:10:28.096 --> 00:10:30.826
Of course, Poland, Lithuania and Latvia

00:10:31.126 --> 00:10:33.206
were catching up from much lower levels.

00:10:33.426 --> 00:10:36.746
But Italy was not just growing slowly.

00:10:36.786 --> 00:10:38.816
It was barely moving at all.

00:10:39.776 --> 00:10:43.516
Now, this has nothing to do with laziness.

00:10:43.516 --> 00:10:46.346
It's not to suggest that Italian workers

00:10:46.346 --> 00:10:48.946
are twiddling their thumbs or going on

00:10:48.946 --> 00:10:50.906
extended breaks while their colleagues

00:10:50.906 --> 00:10:53.146
in Poland or Latvia are rolling up

00:10:53.146 --> 00:10:55.166
their sleeves and putting in the hours.

00:10:56.116 --> 00:10:58.396
The average Italian actually works

00:10:58.436 --> 00:11:00.426
longer hours each year than the

00:11:00.456 --> 00:11:03.446
average German, Frenchman or Spaniard.

00:11:03.856 --> 00:11:06.246
The problem is what each of those

00:11:06.306 --> 00:11:09.106
hours produces, and a lot of that

00:11:09.196 --> 00:11:11.416
depends on things the individual

00:11:11.416 --> 00:11:14.136
worker cannot control, like the size

00:11:14.136 --> 00:11:15.736
of the company they work for, the

00:11:15.736 --> 00:11:17.743
technology they are given, and the

00:11:17.743 --> 00:11:19.946
way that the business is organised.

00:11:20.996 --> 00:11:23.176
And here, we need to talk about

00:11:23.186 --> 00:11:26.026
the set of traps that work together

00:11:26.086 --> 00:11:27.786
to drive down productivity.

00:11:28.606 --> 00:11:30.786
The first trap is size.

00:11:31.286 --> 00:11:33.516
As we talked about a few minutes ago,

00:11:33.906 --> 00:11:36.676
Italy is a country of tiny companies.

00:11:37.116 --> 00:11:39.176
Firms with fewer than ten

00:11:39.206 --> 00:11:41.536
employees provide around forty

00:11:41.615 --> 00:11:43.255
per cent of Italian jobs.

00:11:43.696 --> 00:11:45.656
In Germany and France, the

00:11:45.656 --> 00:11:47.596
figure is about half that.

00:11:48.066 --> 00:11:50.326
Now, there's nothing wrong with small

00:11:50.336 --> 00:11:53.356
companies, of course, but they tend to

00:11:53.356 --> 00:11:56.586
be less productive on a per worker basis.

00:11:56.766 --> 00:11:58.436
They don't have the economies

00:11:58.436 --> 00:12:00.506
of scale of larger companies.

00:12:01.236 --> 00:12:03.386
And many Italian companies

00:12:03.596 --> 00:12:05.626
stay small on purpose.

00:12:05.956 --> 00:12:08.996
Italian bureaucracy is suffocating.

00:12:09.416 --> 00:12:11.776
Regulations become more demanding

00:12:11.966 --> 00:12:14.046
as a company grows, and this

00:12:14.076 --> 00:12:15.736
can discourage owners from

00:12:15.776 --> 00:12:17.326
crossing particular thresholds.

00:12:17.806 --> 00:12:20.296
So business owners may keep

00:12:20.296 --> 00:12:22.276
their firms just below a size

00:12:22.306 --> 00:12:24.096
that brings new obligations.

00:12:25.216 --> 00:12:26.676
There is also the question

00:12:26.876 --> 00:12:28.927
of who runs these companies.

00:12:29.416 --> 00:12:31.886
Most small Italian companies are

00:12:31.926 --> 00:12:34.216
family businesses, and in about

00:12:34.296 --> 00:12:36.686
two-thirds of them, the managers

00:12:36.916 --> 00:12:38.446
are members of the family.

00:12:39.116 --> 00:12:41.206
Again, there is nothing inherently

00:12:41.206 --> 00:12:43.336
wrong with a family business, but

00:12:43.516 --> 00:12:45.656
it tends to mean that the managers

00:12:45.706 --> 00:12:48.066
are chosen because of who their

00:12:48.246 --> 00:12:51.016
parents are rather than whether they

00:12:51.016 --> 00:12:52.786
are the best person to do the job.

00:12:53.646 --> 00:12:57.046
And Italian companies are often hopelessly

00:12:57.046 --> 00:12:59.406
behind when it comes to technology.

00:13:00.386 --> 00:13:03.506
From the 1990s onwards, the companies

00:13:03.506 --> 00:13:05.726
that really grew were the ones that

00:13:05.882 --> 00:13:07.686
reorganised themselves around new

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:09.846
technology: professional managers,

00:13:10.186 --> 00:13:12.346
new systems, constant retraining.

00:13:12.826 --> 00:13:13.586
That kind of stuff.

00:13:14.381 --> 00:13:16.515
These were exactly the things

00:13:16.556 --> 00:13:18.615
that a small, family-run

00:13:18.646 --> 00:13:20.856
firm was least likely to do.

00:13:21.436 --> 00:13:23.726
This is one reason economists

00:13:23.726 --> 00:13:26.066
focus on the timing of Italy's

00:13:26.086 --> 00:13:28.666
productivity freeze in the mid-1990s.

00:13:28.826 --> 00:13:30.336
This was just at the start of

00:13:30.336 --> 00:13:32.756
the Internet and computer age.

00:13:33.696 --> 00:13:36.206
The second trap is the labour

00:13:36.206 --> 00:13:39.196
market, and this one is generational.

00:13:40.126 --> 00:13:43.316
Older Italian workers, the insiders,

00:13:43.636 --> 00:13:45.486
have traditionally enjoyed some of the

00:13:45.516 --> 00:13:48.576
strongest job protections in Europe, and

00:13:48.776 --> 00:13:51.906
it is notoriously difficult to get rid

00:13:52.096 --> 00:13:54.536
of an underperforming employee in Italy.

00:13:55.296 --> 00:13:58.226
This is not to suggest that Italy

00:13:58.226 --> 00:14:00.036
would be in a better place if it

00:14:00.066 --> 00:14:03.016
adopted US-style labour laws, with

00:14:03.256 --> 00:14:05.456
employees being fired for looking at

00:14:05.466 --> 00:14:08.116
the boss the wrong way, but it has

00:14:08.116 --> 00:14:10.926
made companies incredibly nervous about

00:14:10.956 --> 00:14:14.186
hiring anyone on full-time contracts.

00:14:15.946 --> 00:14:18.336
Instead, what happened was the

00:14:18.336 --> 00:14:21.276
mass adoption of short, temporary

00:14:21.276 --> 00:14:23.556
contracts of months at a time.

00:14:24.326 --> 00:14:27.416
The result was a two-tiered system.

00:14:27.886 --> 00:14:30.076
The silver-haired man sitting at

00:14:30.076 --> 00:14:31.796
the cafe, leisurely sipping his

00:14:31.796 --> 00:14:33.616
coffee and reading the newspaper,

00:14:34.011 --> 00:14:35.994
has a job for life, or perhaps he's

00:14:35.994 --> 00:14:38.134
already enjoying a generous pension.

00:14:38.784 --> 00:14:40.774
The thirty-year-old physics graduate

00:14:41.044 --> 00:14:43.464
making his espresso is on a six-month

00:14:43.504 --> 00:14:45.484
temporary contract with a salary

00:14:45.664 --> 00:14:47.094
that barely covers the rent.

00:14:47.944 --> 00:14:50.064
And, of course, an employer does

00:14:50.064 --> 00:14:52.444
not invest in training someone who

00:14:52.483 --> 00:14:54.124
will likely be gone by Christmas.

00:14:54.274 --> 00:14:56.574
A bank does not give a mortgage to

00:14:56.574 --> 00:14:58.414
someone with a six-month contract.

00:14:58.614 --> 00:15:01.364
An entire generation, my generation,

00:15:01.434 --> 00:15:04.124
essentially, was left in this limbo,

00:15:04.514 --> 00:15:06.684
moving from one short-term contract

00:15:06.704 --> 00:15:08.504
to another, and thanking their

00:15:08.504 --> 00:15:10.634
lucky stars if they were able to get

00:15:10.714 --> 00:15:13.414
any sort of permanent, secure job.

00:15:14.524 --> 00:15:17.844
The third productivity trap is an old

00:15:17.844 --> 00:15:19.984
one, one I've spoken about before here,

00:15:20.274 --> 00:15:22.134
and one you are probably aware of:

00:15:22.364 --> 00:15:25.074
the divide between north and south.

00:15:26.264 --> 00:15:28.124
Northern Italy is one of the most

00:15:28.234 --> 00:15:30.694
productive industrial regions in Europe.

00:15:30.964 --> 00:15:34.259
But in the south, so, sort of south

00:15:34.259 --> 00:15:36.284
of Naples and below, it's a very

00:15:36.374 --> 00:15:39.364
different situation, with incomes half

00:15:39.374 --> 00:15:41.454
those in the north and much higher

00:15:41.454 --> 00:15:43.974
levels of unemployment and crime.

00:15:44.474 --> 00:15:48.314
So Italy's national statistics are really

00:15:48.314 --> 00:15:50.664
an average of two different economies:

00:15:50.994 --> 00:15:53.904
the north is doing just about OK, given

00:15:53.904 --> 00:15:55.674
all of the issues we've currently spoken

00:15:55.674 --> 00:15:59.164
about, but the country overall is dragged

00:15:59.214 --> 00:16:01.873
down significantly, from an economic

00:16:01.893 --> 00:16:03.733
perspective at least, by the south.

00:16:04.717 --> 00:16:07.514
And sitting on top of everything

00:16:07.794 --> 00:16:10.324
is the fourth trap: the debt.

00:16:11.294 --> 00:16:13.814
That mountain of borrowing from

00:16:13.814 --> 00:16:16.713
the 1980s never really went away.

00:16:17.064 --> 00:16:18.884
New debt was issued to

00:16:18.884 --> 00:16:20.314
roll over the old debt.

00:16:20.924 --> 00:16:22.914
Every year, Italy has to

00:16:22.914 --> 00:16:24.464
pay interest on these debts.

00:16:24.874 --> 00:16:27.754
And that bill is massive,

00:16:28.114 --> 00:16:30.674
€90 billion every year.

00:16:31.204 --> 00:16:33.554
And that's just the interest payments

00:16:33.624 --> 00:16:36.454
— that isn't repaying any of the debt,

00:16:36.734 --> 00:16:38.104
like you might do with a home loan;

00:16:38.334 --> 00:16:40.574
it's literally just paying the bill

00:16:40.844 --> 00:16:43.774
for decisions taken 30 or 40 years ago.

00:16:44.924 --> 00:16:47.454
Now, Italy isn't the only country

00:16:47.454 --> 00:16:50.374
with huge public debt — the UK is

00:16:50.374 --> 00:16:52.884
in a not dissimilar situation — but

00:16:53.224 --> 00:16:56.444
clearly it handcuffs the policymaking

00:16:56.444 --> 00:16:57.774
decisions of any government.

00:16:58.184 --> 00:17:00.674
€90 billion that you need to pay your

00:17:00.704 --> 00:17:03.864
creditors is €90 billion you can't

00:17:03.864 --> 00:17:06.944
spend on education or healthcare, and

00:17:07.364 --> 00:17:11.344
in fact, Italy spends almost exactly

00:17:11.554 --> 00:17:14.214
the same amount of money on its interest

00:17:14.224 --> 00:17:17.424
payments as it does on public education.

00:17:18.284 --> 00:17:21.714
Those freebies in the 1980s, well, now

00:17:21.714 --> 00:17:23.904
Italians are having to pay the bill.

00:17:24.774 --> 00:17:27.563
Now, it is one thing to describe all

00:17:27.603 --> 00:17:30.244
of this with charts and percentages

00:17:30.254 --> 00:17:32.884
and talk of GDP and productivity.

00:17:33.294 --> 00:17:35.874
It is another thing to live it.

00:17:36.154 --> 00:17:38.514
So, I want you to close your

00:17:38.514 --> 00:17:40.734
eyes and imagine someone.

00:17:41.364 --> 00:17:43.414
Imagine a young Italian

00:17:43.414 --> 00:17:46.224
woman, born in the late 1980s.

00:17:46.644 --> 00:17:48.274
She does everything right.

00:17:48.754 --> 00:17:50.884
She studies hard, goes to university,

00:17:51.144 --> 00:17:53.334
and graduates with a good degree.

00:17:53.874 --> 00:17:55.984
She goes abroad to continue her

00:17:55.994 --> 00:17:58.024
studies, to the UK, let's say.

00:17:58.584 --> 00:18:00.654
She would love to return to Italy,

00:18:01.104 --> 00:18:04.194
but there are no jobs, at least none

00:18:04.194 --> 00:18:06.154
in a field she wants to work in.

00:18:06.884 --> 00:18:09.154
The best thing she could get might be a

00:18:09.154 --> 00:18:12.004
series of low-paid temporary contracts.

00:18:12.554 --> 00:18:15.044
She is offered a job in another

00:18:15.044 --> 00:18:17.754
European country, a permanent job with

00:18:17.754 --> 00:18:20.264
a good salary and a clear career path.

00:18:20.914 --> 00:18:22.594
Of course, she takes it.

00:18:23.164 --> 00:18:24.884
Still, she keeps her eye on

00:18:24.914 --> 00:18:26.614
opportunities back in Italy.

00:18:27.104 --> 00:18:30.534
The years go by, and now even with 10

00:18:30.544 --> 00:18:32.854
years' experience in her field, she might

00:18:32.854 --> 00:18:35.494
have to wait until she is in her mid-50s

00:18:35.674 --> 00:18:38.124
to be considered "experienced" enough for

00:18:38.124 --> 00:18:41.574
a similar job back in Italy, and even if

00:18:41.614 --> 00:18:44.424
she were offered a job, it would be at a

00:18:44.434 --> 00:18:46.894
fraction of what she was earning abroad.

00:18:47.874 --> 00:18:50.644
Perhaps you know someone like this.

00:18:50.884 --> 00:18:52.034
Perhaps you work with them.

00:18:52.664 --> 00:18:55.484
The person I just described is someone

00:18:55.484 --> 00:18:57.304
who is quite easy for me to imagine.

00:18:57.784 --> 00:18:59.604
She is my wife, and she is

00:18:59.604 --> 00:19:01.394
one of 6 and a half million

00:19:01.394 --> 00:19:03.444
Italians who are living abroad.

00:19:04.534 --> 00:19:06.824
There is an expression in Italian

00:19:06.824 --> 00:19:09.844
for this: la fuga di cervelli, brain

00:19:09.854 --> 00:19:11.344
drain, you might say in English.

00:19:12.194 --> 00:19:14.853
On an emotional level, it's

00:19:14.894 --> 00:19:16.214
a sad thing for a country.

00:19:16.533 --> 00:19:19.334
it's a sad thing for Italian parents

00:19:19.394 --> 00:19:20.964
to see their children move abroad.

00:19:21.154 --> 00:19:23.184
But purely on an economic

00:19:23.184 --> 00:19:24.634
level, it's awful.

00:19:25.144 --> 00:19:28.134
This person receives a tax-funded

00:19:28.264 --> 00:19:30.954
education, from nursery right through

00:19:30.954 --> 00:19:33.024
to a university degree, and then

00:19:33.244 --> 00:19:35.764
just as they are in a position to

00:19:35.764 --> 00:19:38.254
start contributing, they move abroad

00:19:38.474 --> 00:19:41.664
and pay taxes in the UK, Germany,

00:19:42.074 --> 00:19:43.944
or, in the case of my wife, Sweden.

00:19:44.664 --> 00:19:48.094
Meanwhile, back in Italy, partly given

00:19:48.094 --> 00:19:50.554
the precarious economic situation that

00:19:50.554 --> 00:19:52.354
most young people find themselves in,

00:19:52.884 --> 00:19:55.524
fewer and fewer babies are being born.

00:19:56.654 --> 00:20:01.544
In 2025, 355,000 births

00:20:01.564 --> 00:20:02.794
were registered in Italy.

00:20:03.204 --> 00:20:05.534
That is the lowest number since

00:20:05.554 --> 00:20:08.624
Italian unification in 1861, when

00:20:08.624 --> 00:20:10.664
the country had fewer than half

00:20:10.704 --> 00:20:12.584
as many people as it does today.

00:20:13.724 --> 00:20:16.504
And this younger generation is

00:20:16.504 --> 00:20:18.374
the generation that will be paying

00:20:18.384 --> 00:20:20.304
for the generous pensions of

00:20:20.304 --> 00:20:21.774
their parents and grandparents.

00:20:22.534 --> 00:20:25.054
And there simply aren't enough of them.

00:20:25.784 --> 00:20:28.184
In my wife's hometown, there

00:20:28.184 --> 00:20:30.344
are more people who are older

00:20:30.394 --> 00:20:33.634
than 75 than there are under 18.

00:20:34.714 --> 00:20:38.354
So, joining the dots together, on one

00:20:38.354 --> 00:20:40.604
side you have young people leaving

00:20:40.894 --> 00:20:43.264
and fewer babies being born, and on

00:20:43.264 --> 00:20:45.094
the other side you have an ageing

00:20:45.094 --> 00:20:47.334
population with increasing care

00:20:47.394 --> 00:20:49.914
costs and generous pensions to fund.

00:20:50.854 --> 00:20:53.404
The tax burden on the working population

00:20:53.594 --> 00:20:56.004
is already one of the highest in Europe,

00:20:56.404 --> 00:20:59.874
and four out of every ten euros spent

00:20:59.904 --> 00:21:02.174
by the Italian state goes to either

00:21:02.234 --> 00:21:04.784
interest on its debts or pensions.

00:21:05.723 --> 00:21:09.564
It is a vicious circle, and try as various

00:21:09.594 --> 00:21:11.934
Italian governments might, it's incredibly

00:21:11.934 --> 00:21:13.744
difficult to do anything about it.

00:21:14.304 --> 00:21:16.237
So, can anything be done?

00:21:16.823 --> 00:21:19.123
Or is Italy simply doomed

00:21:19.154 --> 00:21:21.574
to a gentle, sunlit decline?

00:21:22.844 --> 00:21:24.574
To be fair, the recent

00:21:24.574 --> 00:21:26.714
picture is not all bleak.

00:21:27.174 --> 00:21:29.394
Italy has just been through an

00:21:29.404 --> 00:21:31.614
unusually stable political period.

00:21:32.074 --> 00:21:34.514
Financial markets spent years treating

00:21:34.524 --> 00:21:37.084
Italy as the eurozone's greatest risk,

00:21:37.354 --> 00:21:39.823
but they have changed their tune slightly.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:43.884
There has been some modest employment

00:21:43.904 --> 00:21:46.294
reform, and unemployment is down.

00:21:46.784 --> 00:21:49.284
Italy, for all its woes, is

00:21:49.284 --> 00:21:51.224
still Europe's second-largest

00:21:51.224 --> 00:21:53.394
manufacturing country, after Germany.

00:21:54.214 --> 00:21:55.644
And then there is the money.

00:21:56.654 --> 00:21:59.404
After the pandemic, there were a series

00:21:59.404 --> 00:22:02.054
of domestic and EU recovery measures,

00:22:02.134 --> 00:22:05.324
with hundreds of billions of euros being

00:22:05.334 --> 00:22:07.654
dished out for everything from digital

00:22:07.684 --> 00:22:09.954
infrastructure to building subsidies.

00:22:10.504 --> 00:22:12.764
Whether all that money has been spent

00:22:12.764 --> 00:22:15.734
well is something of an open question, but

00:22:16.024 --> 00:22:18.824
there was this huge cash injection that

00:22:18.824 --> 00:22:20.984
has given the country a serious boost.

00:22:22.124 --> 00:22:24.394
Now, all of those things I

00:22:24.394 --> 00:22:25.654
mentioned at the start of the

00:22:25.654 --> 00:22:28.164
episode, the natural advantages

00:22:28.174 --> 00:22:30.474
Italy has, they are still there.

00:22:31.054 --> 00:22:33.554
But so is everything else: the

00:22:33.814 --> 00:22:36.164
strangulating bureaucracy, the short

00:22:36.164 --> 00:22:39.444
contracts, the flat wages, the gargantuan

00:22:39.444 --> 00:22:42.054
public debt, and we haven't even

00:22:42.054 --> 00:22:44.884
mentioned the informal, under-the-table

00:22:44.884 --> 00:22:47.564
economy, which is estimated to be worth

00:22:47.814 --> 00:22:50.974
something like 10% of all Italian GDP

00:22:51.294 --> 00:22:54.404
and costs the Italian state up to €100

00:22:54.474 --> 00:22:57.394
billion a year in lost tax revenue.

00:22:57.884 --> 00:23:00.644
Or the vast problem of organised crime.

00:23:01.634 --> 00:23:03.964
Italy is a wonderful place.

00:23:04.484 --> 00:23:06.114
That is, of course, my opinion,

00:23:06.464 --> 00:23:08.094
but I imagine it's an opinion

00:23:08.094 --> 00:23:10.474
shared by most of the 60 million

00:23:10.504 --> 00:23:12.544
tourists that come there every year.

00:23:13.124 --> 00:23:15.394
Italian city centres and hilltop

00:23:15.424 --> 00:23:17.714
villages are beautiful, Italian food

00:23:17.714 --> 00:23:20.634
is fantastic, and to the outside eye,

00:23:20.824 --> 00:23:22.514
it looks like everything is going

00:23:22.524 --> 00:23:25.244
swimmingly, like life could not be better.

00:23:26.164 --> 00:23:28.804
Under the bonnet, however, the economy

00:23:29.154 --> 00:23:32.254
is a mess, dragged down by the past and

00:23:32.254 --> 00:23:34.874
throttled by a dozen different factors.

00:23:35.544 --> 00:23:38.644
It's the home of Ferrari, Lamborghini, and

00:23:38.644 --> 00:23:41.194
a plethora of other feats of engineering.

00:23:41.804 --> 00:23:43.704
But until something about the

00:23:43.704 --> 00:23:46.134
economy changes, the Italian economic

00:23:46.194 --> 00:23:48.894
engine will keep idling, running

00:23:49.204 --> 00:23:51.044
but going nowhere, in a beautiful

00:23:51.044 --> 00:23:53.264
country that deserves much better.

00:23:55.804 --> 00:23:58.364
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:23:58.404 --> 00:24:00.284
on what's wrong with Italy's economy.

00:24:00.614 --> 00:24:02.662
For the Italians out there, I

00:24:02.662 --> 00:24:04.442
hope you'll think this was a fair

00:24:04.442 --> 00:24:06.332
assessment of your wonderful country.

00:24:06.872 --> 00:24:08.532
As always, I would love to know

00:24:08.532 --> 00:24:10.152
what you thought of this episode.

00:24:10.462 --> 00:24:12.072
Let me know in the comments below,

00:24:12.332 --> 00:24:13.792
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:24:13.802 --> 00:24:15.492
where you can comment, and for the

00:24:15.492 --> 00:24:17.212
members among you, you can head right

00:24:17.212 --> 00:24:19.312
into our community forum, which is at

00:24:19.312 --> 00:24:22.002
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:24:22.002 --> 00:24:24.172
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:24:24.492 --> 00:24:26.292
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:26.292 --> 00:24:28.992
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:29.282 --> 00:24:32.102
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:32.102 --> 00:24:34.052
I'll catch you in the next episode.

